The Human-English claims that a product of domains must be built from data objects, not taken as the cartesian product of elements: a token records partial information about one coordinate, so knowledge of \(X\) about the first coordinate of a pair and of \(Y\) about the second is carried by separate tokens, and that Definitions 6.1 and 6.3 together with Propositions 6.2 and 6.4 characterize \(\mathbf{A}\times\mathbf{B}\) and \(\mathbf{A}+\mathbf{B}\) uniquely up to isomorphism, supply the maps that make product and sum functors, and give \(\mathbf{BOOL}=\mathbf{1}+\mathbf{1}\) two elements \(\mathrm{true}\) and \(\mathrm{false}\) such that an approximable map on \(\mathbf{BOOL}\) is uniquely determined by its values on \(\mathrm{true}\), \(\mathrm{false}\), and \(\bot_{\mathbf{BOOL}}\), the first two of which may be chosen arbitrarily. The imported Lean closure constructs the data-object product, the separated sum, and the unit, and contains none of the isomorphism, functor, or \(\mathbf{BOOL}\) statements. On the product, `IsProdToken` holds of a pair when the first component is the least token of the first system or the second component is the least token of the second, `prodBot` is the pair of those two least tokens, `fstFinset` retains first components of tokens whose second component is least, and `sndFinset` retains second components of tokens whose first component is least, so the shared bottom belongs to both projections; `ProdCon` requires both projections consistent, and `ProdEnt` requires that consistency together with entailment of the first component from the left projection whenever the second component is least and of the second component from the right projection whenever the first component is least. The proof in `productSystem` checks the six information-system axioms by monotonicity of the projections and downward closure of consistency for subsets, by the singleton cases in which the first component is least, the second is least, or both are least, by preservation of each projection on insertion of an entailed token, by entailment of the product bottom because each factor entails its own bottom, by reflexivity on the relevant projection for a member, and by cut in each factor for transitivity. On the sum, `SumToken` is the inductive type with left, right, and bottom constructors, separated by `instDecidableEqSumToken`, and `sumBot` is the bottom constructor; `lftFinset` and `rhtFinset` extract the matching component and ignore bottom, as in `lftFinset.insert_bot`, `rhtFinset.insert_bot`, `lftFinset.singleton_bot`, and `rhtFinset.singleton_bot`. `SumCon` is the disjunction of “left projection consistent in \(\mathbf{A}\) and right projection empty” with “left projection empty and right projection consistent in \(\mathbf{B}\)”, and `SumCon.lft_con_of_lft_nonempty`, `SumCon.rht_empty_of_lft_nonempty`, `SumCon.rht_con_of_rht_nonempty`, and `SumCon.lft_empty_of_rht_nonempty` discharge the four consequences of a nonempty side. `SumEnt` holds of bottom from every sum-consistent set, and holds of a left or right token only when that projection is nonempty and the summand entails the component. The proof in `sumSystem` uses monotonicity and inheritance of an empty projection for subsets, the three singleton computations, the fact that inserting bottom changes neither projection while inserting a left token has already forced the right projection to be empty, reflexivity from a nonempty consistent projection, and transitivity reduced to transitivity in the inhabited summand. The unit, which is the summand \(\mathbf{1}\) in the chapter’s formula for \(\mathbf{BOOL}\) but is not that sum, is `unitSystem`: its only token is least, every finite set is consistent, and every token is entailed, so each axiom is immediate; `unitElement_eq_bot` shows there is only the bottom element by antisymmetry; `approxMap_from_unit_eq_const` rewrites every element as bottom and uses uniqueness of constant maps; `approxMap_to_unit_eq_const` uses that every image is bottom; and `toElement_constMap_bot`, which is the numbered `theorem_65`, is only the identity that the constant map at an element \(b\) sends the bottom element of the unit to \(b\). The remaining projection lemmas, including `mem_fstFinset`, `mem_sndFinset`, `mem_lftFinset`, `mem_rhtFinset`, `fstFinset.funion`, and `rhtFinset.funion`, are the calculations those axiom proofs call, and they do not produce a pairing, a copairing, or a two-element sum. **Theorem:** partial — `productSystem` and `sumSystem` are the data-object product and the separated sum, but uniqueness up to isomorphism, functoriality, and the determination of maps on \(\mathbf{BOOL}=\mathbf{1}+\mathbf{1}\) are absent, and `theorem_65` is the constant-map evaluation identity rather than that \(\mathbf{BOOL}\) claim. **Proof:** no — the chapter gives no argument, and the Lean proofs are the axiom checks in `productSystem`, `sumSystem`, and `unitSystem`, not a proof that the product of elements is the expected one or that maps on \(\mathbf{BOOL}\) are fixed by the values on \(\mathrm{true}\), \(\mathrm{false}\), and bottom.
